commenti.body:
Either you grew up as a working class dirtbag in Eastern Canada, or you don't understand Goin' Down the Road. It doesn't matter that the film is now 50+ years old - I've met these people. The principle characters of the film are just younger versions of the lifelong boomer labourers that I knew and worked with when I was old enough, or just slightly dated older models of the guys who languished in my hometown. There is a quintessentially Canadian desperation, a grey oppressive looming existential darkness that you can only find in a dilapidated South Ontarian rural town deprived of it's main industry or in the shadier outskirts of a large Canadian city. It's 90 minutes of watching the Canadian labourers' personal fantasy get slowly crushed, only for them to jump to the next one oblivious to the pattern they are trapped in. You need money, so leave the Maritimes for Toronto. The Big Smoke didn't treat you right, so you leave for the West and try to get a job as a rig pig. You might come back to Ontario and work as a furniture mover for 30 years, you might make enough scratch in Alberta to be happy, you might end up like my uncle - migrated West until he couldn't get further, drowned himself in booze in a small town in B.C. working at a cannery and lamented how nothing never went so right for him. 

&nbsp;

It's a depressing and starkly real depiction of a side of Canada that people from other countries don't understand. There's a type of unique malaise that can infect you here as you move city to city, hoping the next one won't be a cold, grey and hopless as the last. You end up living for the next supposed utopia. "Oh, if I move to Toronto, things will be different", "There's more opportunity in Calgary", "I was always a BC person anyway", "If I move to Montreal, then I'll be happy.", etc.
